@*
    ONE LABEL CHIP - story 2.4. UX-DR5, UX-DR8, UX-DR13, UX-DR40; DESIGN.md:180-183, :451, :457-462.

    NOT A CONTROL: a span holding the Label's name, filled from the palette through its key's modifier class
    (`label-chip--{key}` over `var(--label-{key})`), never an inline style, so no colour is stated outside the token
    layer and the palette gate's figures are what renders. One ink in both themes (`--label-ink`, story 2.4 open
    question 4). The 2px radius, the meta type role, no fixed height and no shadow: the chip sizes to its text, so text
    spacing and 200% text zoom grow it. The name is user data, never transformed.

    NOTHING DEPENDS ON COLOUR: the name is always the chip's text.
*@

<span class="type-meta label-chip label-chip--@ColourClass">@Name</span>

@code {
    /// <summary>The Label's name.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The Label's palette key, such as <c>rust</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string Colour { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    // A key the client does not know draws the chip unfilled rather than with a class the token layer lacks.
    private string ColourClass => Yello.Contracts.Labels.LabelColours.Keys.Contains(Colour, StringComparer.Ordinal) ? Colour : "unknown";
}
